  {
    "client_msg_id": "9c80fe8f-bfa5-419b-9fb2-a1bb920bc818",
    "type": "message",
    "user": "U06M6E5J2SC",
    "text": "Returning handled/not_handled values is typical for the Chain of Command design pattern. In such a case, the dispatch would be implemented like:\n```handled_t dispatch(self, event) {\n  if self.event_for_us(event) {\n     return handled;\n  }\n  for ch in child_listeners {\n     if ch.dispatch(event) == handled {\n        return handled;\n     }\n  }\n  return not_handled\n}```\nFor instance, with such a pattern you would typically implement HTML/JS dispatching a click event (x, y) to an interested DOM element. Are you asking for a case in which a DOM element would always return `handled` disregarding if the event is even at its bounding-box?",
    "ts": "1748709017.109649",
    "team": "TV7BVTSJK",
